Subject: vm: wait on host descriptors from the thread that needs them

A guest thread blocked on a socket is woken through the shared event
selector: the kernel wakes the selector's poll thread, which then wakes
the guest thread, so one readiness event requires a handoff through the
selector thread. This adds a wait that lets the thread block on the
descriptors itself; no syscall uses it yet, so every guest behaves
exactly as it did.

`try_suspend_on_pollfd` in lib/vm/src/trap/suspend.rs is the descriptor
counterpart of the direct futex wait: `ppoll(2)` in
lib/vm/src/trap/pollfd.rs over the descriptors the caller named plus one
interrupt eventfd per thread, on the caller's own OS thread whether or
not it can suspend. The wait sits outside the active section, so it
holds no freeze off; the interrupt and freeze-gate generations end it,
and whoever changes one calls `wake_host_pollfd`, because a thread in
the kernel sees neither a changed word nor a waker. A thread retiring
into a freeze runs no destructor, so its eventfd is closed for it.

The caller reads the result out of the array it handed over: every
`HostPollFd` carries the events seen, and one that reported a peer's
half-close or hangup also says whether bytes are still unread, which is
what tells a last request from an end of stream. One wait names at most
`HOST_POLL_FDS_MAX` (64) descriptors and refuses a larger set rather
than truncating it. A wait suspended across a fork is refused too:
`POLLFD_EPOCH` moves when the coroutines are resurrected in a child
whose networking was rebuilt, and the caller resolves its descriptors
again.

Every socket backend gains `host_fd()`: a connected stream, a listener
and a bound datagram socket answer with their descriptor, everything
else with `None`. The kernel is the only place readiness lives, so a
wait on the descriptor sees everything there is.

The wait is available on Linux, unless the process started with
DISABLE_DIRECT_POLL set, logged at warning level on the first wait, or a
wait could not interpret its own result and turned it off for good;
elsewhere every wait takes the existing path.

